# Experiment: Implementation of Elastic Load Balancer with Auto Scaling Using AWS EC2 Instances

## Aim

To implement an Application Load Balancer (ALB) with Auto Scaling Group (ASG) using AWS EC2 instances for automatic traffic distribution, high availability, and automatic scaling based on demand. 

---

# Procedure

# Part A — Create EC2 Web Servers

## Step 1: Launch EC2 Instance 1

1. Login to Amazon Web Services AWS Management Console.
2. Go to:

   * EC2 → Launch Instance
3. Configure:

| Field         | Value          |
| ------------- | -------------- |
| Name          | webserver-1    |
| AMI           | Amazon Linux 2 |
| Instance Type | t2.micro       |

4. Configure Security Group:

| Rule | Port | Source   |
| ---- | ---- | -------- |
| SSH  | 22   | My IP    |
| HTTP | 80   | Anywhere |

5. Create/select key pair.
6. Launch instance.

---

## Step 2: Install Apache Web Server on Instance 1

Connect to instance using SSH and run:

```bash id="r5j7t1"
yum update -y
yum install httpd -y
systemctl start httpd
systemctl enable httpd
echo "This is Server 1" > /var/www/html/index.html
```

---

## Step 3: Launch EC2 Instance 2

Repeat same steps and configure:

| Field | Value       |
| ----- | ----------- |
| Name  | webserver-2 |

---

## Step 4: Install Apache on Instance 2

Run:

```bash id="a8m2v6"
yum update -y
yum install httpd -y
systemctl start httpd
systemctl enable httpd
echo "This is Server 2" > /var/www/html/index.html
```

---

# Part B — Create Application Load Balancer (ALB)

## Step 5: Create Security Group for Load Balancer

1. Go to:

   * EC2 → Security Groups
2. Click:

   * Create Security Group
3. Configure:

| Field | Value |
| ----- | ----- |
| Name  | lb-sg |

Add inbound rules:

| Rule             | Port | Source   |
| ---------------- | ---- | -------- |
| HTTP             | 80   | Anywhere |
| HTTPS (Optional) | 443  | Anywhere |

4. Save security group.

---

## Step 6: Create Load Balancer

1. Go to:

   * EC2 → Load Balancers
2. Click:

   * Create Load Balancer
3. Select:

   * Application Load Balancer

---

## Step 7: Configure Load Balancer

| Field           | Value           |
| --------------- | --------------- |
| Name            | my-alb          |
| Scheme          | Internet-facing |
| IP Address Type | IPv4            |
| Listener        | HTTP (80)       |

Select:

* Default VPC
* Multiple subnets for high availability

Attach security group:

```text
lb-sg
```

---

## Step 8: Create Target Group

1. Create new target group.

| Field       | Value          |
| ----------- | -------------- |
| Name        | web-servers-tg |
| Target Type | Instance       |
| Protocol    | HTTP           |
| Port        | 80             |

2. Health Check Path:

   ```text
   /
   ```

---

## Step 9: Register EC2 Targets

1. Select:

   * webserver-1
   * webserver-2
2. Click:

   * Include as Pending
3. Click:

   * Register Targets

---

## Step 10: Create Load Balancer

1. Review settings.
2. Click:

   * Create Load Balancer
3. Wait until ALB becomes active.

---

# Step 11: Verify Load Balancer

1. Open:

   * Load Balancers
2. Copy DNS Name.
3. Open DNS in browser.

Refresh multiple times to observe traffic switching between:

* Server 1
* Server 2

---

# Step 12: Verify Health Checks

1. Open:

   * Target Groups → Targets
2. Verify instances show:

   ```text
   Healthy
   ```

---

# Part C — Configure Auto Scaling Group

## Step 13: Create AMI from Existing Instance

1. Go to:

   * EC2 → Instances
2. Select configured web server instance.
3. Click:

   * Actions → Image and Templates → Create Image
4. Configure:

| Field      | Value            |
| ---------- | ---------------- |
| Image Name | my-webserver-AMI |

5. Create image.
6. Wait until AMI status becomes:

   ```text
   Available
   ```

---

## Step 14: Create Launch Template

1. Go to:

   * EC2 → Launch Templates
2. Click:

   * Create Launch Template

Configure:

| Field          | Value              |
| -------------- | ------------------ |
| Template Name  | my-launch-template |
| AMI            | my-webserver-AMI   |
| Instance Type  | t2.micro           |
| Security Group | lb-sg              |

3. Select existing key pair.
4. Click:

   * Create Launch Template

---

# Step 15: Create Auto Scaling Group

1. Go to:

   * EC2 → Auto Scaling Groups
2. Click:

   * Create Auto Scaling Group

Configure:

| Field           | Value              |
| --------------- | ------------------ |
| Name            | webserver-asg      |
| Launch Template | my-launch-template |

---

# Step 16: Configure Network and Load Balancer

1. Select:

   * Default VPC
   * Multiple subnets

2. Enable:

   * Attach to existing load balancer

3. Select:

   ```text
   my-alb
   ```

4. Health Check Type:

   ```text
   HTTP
   ```

5. Grace Period:

   ```text
   300 seconds
   ```

---

# Step 17: Configure Scaling Capacity

| Parameter        | Value |
| ---------------- | ----- |
| Desired Capacity | 2     |
| Minimum Capacity | 1     |
| Maximum Capacity | 4     |

Click:

* Next → Create Auto Scaling Group

---

# Step 18: Verify Auto Scaling

1. Open:

   * Auto Scaling Groups → Instances
2. Verify instances launch automatically.
3. Terminate one instance manually.
4. Observe:

   * ASG launches replacement instance automatically.

---

# Step 19: Test Load Balancer with Auto Scaling

1. Open ALB DNS Name in browser.
2. Refresh multiple times.
3. Verify traffic distributed across healthy instances.

---

# Observation

* Two EC2 web servers created successfully.
* Application Load Balancer distributed traffic between servers.
* Health checks monitored server availability.
* Auto Scaling Group launched and replaced EC2 instances automatically.
* Load balancing and scaling worked successfully under varying load conditions. 

---

# Result

Successfully implemented an Application Load Balancer with Auto Scaling Group using AWS EC2 instances for automatic traffic distribution, high availability, fault tolerance, and dynamic scaling. 
